<BaseLayout hreflang={false}
  title="Shipping from China to Ireland: Cost, Dublin Port & 23% VAT"
  description="China to Ireland freight: sea and air cost, Dublin (IEDUB) and Cork (IEORK) ports, ~32–43 day transit, 23% VAT on EU duty, UK landbridge trade-off."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Shipping from China to Ireland' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Shipping from China to Ireland: cost, Dublin Port &amp; 23% VAT</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A one-page freight brief for importers moving cargo from Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan, Shenzhen
        and the wider China origin set to Dublin, Cork and the Irish market — with the island
        geography and its short-sea leg, the roughly 32–43-day sea window via the northern range,
        the post-Brexit UK-landbridge trade-off, EU common duty plus 23% VAT, the EORI gate, and the
        hidden short-sea and destination charges that shape the Irish landed cost.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tldr">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Ireland is the EU&rsquo;s Atlantic island — every China shipment ends with a <strong>short-sea crossing</strong> (Irish or Celtic Sea) that continental markets never pay, reached either as a northern-range transshipment feeding west or a direct lo-lo call. The statutory math compounds: 5% duty + 23% VAT = 1.05 × 1.23 = <strong>×1.2915</strong>, so a $10,000 CIF becomes <strong>$12,915</strong> before the feeder leg and destination fees — <strong>$420 higher</strong> than Germany&rsquo;s 19% VAT on the same base. Sea runs <strong>32–43 days</strong> door-to-port, and there is <strong>no practical rail lane</strong> — the New Silk Road stops on the continent, leaving Ireland a sea-versus-air market.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning notes. Ireland&rsquo;s FCL/LCL
        benchmarks come from the WorldFreightHub Europe route data Rotterdam northern-range corridor
        (MEDIUM) and are labelled as reference only, while Dublin/Cork-specific, feeder, air, express and
        DDP dollar figures were not published in the snapshot and are shown as request-for-quote markers.
        Treat every LOW-confidence figure as an indicative planning input and verify with your carrier
        before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Ireland import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">EU common duty 0–12% <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
