  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Ireland deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Ireland is the EU&rsquo;s Atlantic island, and the difference from Germany or the Netherlands is
      structural, not cosmetic. The country data shows <strong>Dublin</strong> as the capital, the
      currency as <strong>EUR</strong> — inside the eurozone, unlike Sweden&rsquo;s SEK — and a
      <strong>23% standard VAT</strong> layered on top of the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>,
      a duty line that is uniform across every member state and varies only by HS code, typically
      <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gate is not the duty rate; it is the <strong>geography</strong>. Ireland is an island, so a
      China container must cross the Irish Sea or Celtic Sea at the end of the voyage — a leg no
      continental destination ever pays. The crossing happens one of three ways: a direct lo-lo
      deep-sea call to Dublin or Cork (thinner than the northern range), a northern-range transshipment
      that feeds west from Rotterdam or Antwerp (the norm), or — the pre-Brexit default now fading — the
      <strong>UK landbridge</strong> across Britain. That final leg, not the headline ocean quote, is
      where the Irish landed cost is actually decided.
    </p>
    <p>
      Brexit changed the arithmetic of the third option. Routing through the UK now means clearing
      <strong>two customs regimes</strong> — EU entry plus UK transit — where a direct Dublin or Cork
      call clears one, which is why direct continental lo-lo services to Irish ports have grown since
      2021. On the tax side, Ireland&rsquo;s 23% VAT sits four points above Germany&rsquo;s 19%, and
      because VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, that gap compounds rather than adds. The
      honest position on pricing is that <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are published only as a
      northern-range Rotterdam reference</strong> (MEDIUM confidence), while Dublin/Cork-specific,
      feeder, air, express and DDP dollar figures are not — so those lines stay explicit
      request-for-quote markers instead of borrowed numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Indicative freight rates from China to Ireland</h2>
      <p>
        Ireland inherits the published <strong>northern-range Rotterdam benchmark</strong> from the
        WorldFreightHub Europe route data, and it is important to read those figures for what they are —
        a corridor reference, not an Irish-port rate. The snapshot carries <strong>$1,200–$4,800 for a
        20ft</strong>, <strong>$1,600–$6,800 for a 40ft</strong> and <strong>$60–$160 per CBM for
        LCL</strong>, all MEDIUM confidence. Dublin/Cork-specific figures are <strong>not published</strong>,
        and the short-sea feeder, air, express and DDP lines were also not published, so those stay LOW
        with a request-for-quote marker.
      </p>

