  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Transit times by origin port and mode</h2>
    <p>
      The China-to-Ireland clock has three beats for ocean cargo: the <strong>deep-sea leg to the
      northern range</strong>, the <strong>short-sea feeder west</strong>, and then the terminal
      handling. The WorldFreightHub Europe route data puts the deep-sea leg at roughly
      <strong>30–38 days</strong> from Shanghai or Ningbo-Zhoushan to Rotterdam or Antwerp, with
      <strong>~1–3 days</strong> of feeder across the Irish Sea to Dublin or Cork — a planning total of
      roughly <strong>32–43 days door-to-port</strong>. Direct lo-lo calls to Dublin or Cork are thinner
      and can trim or match that window depending on the carrier. Rail from Xi&rsquo;an or Chengdu reaches
      the German and Polish termini in roughly <strong>18–22 days</strong>, but there is no continuous
      rail lane to Ireland, so the final leg is a ferry or feeder crossing that erodes the speed
      advantage. Irish air transit is not published, so that line stays explicit instead of borrowing a
      neighbouring airport figure.
    </p>

