      <strong>Sea planning default:</strong> Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan and Shenzhen to Dublin/Cork are
      recorded at roughly 32–43 days door-to-port on the current routing, with the northern-range
      transshipment plus a short-sea feeder as the norm. Both legs are planning estimates, not carrier
      promises — for air, express or a specific door-to-door move, request a routing quote before
      committing to a date. Remember the rail caveat: China–Europe rail runs 1435 mm standard gauge →
      1520 mm broad gauge → back to 1435 mm at the EU border, and even after that it stops on the
      continent — Ireland is reached by sea from that point, not by rail.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. Sea vs air: Ireland&rsquo;s door-to-door decision (no rail middle)</h2>
      <p>
        Ireland collapses the usual three-way choice to two, because the rail option does not exist. The
        New Silk Road terminates at German and Polish hubs, and any final rail move to Ireland still ends
        in a ferry or feeder crossing that erases the speed gain and adds a handling. The honest comparison
        is therefore <strong>sea versus air</strong>: roughly <strong>32–43 days</strong> by sea
        door-to-port against a much faster air move into Dublin Airport — with no verified Irish air
        transit or rate published, so that side of the comparison stays a request-for-quote line.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'mode', label: 'Mode' },
          { key: 'bestFor', label: 'Best for' },
          { key: 'indicativeTime', label: 'Indicative time' },
