      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Critical point — air does not pay for itself on financing alone:</strong> at an 8% cost of
        capital a $50,000 container costs ≈ <strong>$11/day</strong> in transit, so even ~35 days of sea
        transit is only about <strong>$385 of financing</strong> — which does not begin to cover the air
        premium. The sea-versus-air break-even is a <strong>value-of-time</strong> call: air wins only when
        the cost of the delay — stockout, a missed launch or seasonal window, or lost margin on a
        high-value consignment — exceeds the air premium. Rule of thumb: the more of the cargo&rsquo;s
        value you lose per day of delay, the more the ~35 days you buy back by air is worth paying for;
        the more commodity-like the cargo, the more the sea routing wins by default.
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3 mt-2">
        <Card title="Choose sea when...">
          <ul>
            <li>Cost per kg matters more than the ~32–43-day window plus the short-sea feeder.</li>
            <li>The cargo is heavy, palletised or container-sized and can plan ahead.</li>
            <li>You accept the northern-range transshipment and its feeder add-on.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose air when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The cargo is time-critical, light or high-value and Dublin (DUB) is the market.</li>
            <li>The value of a missed sale or launch exceeds the air premium.</li>
            <li>You have a per-kg quote that beats the cost of ~35 days of delay.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Do not plan on rail when...">
          <ul>
            <li>You assume a China–Europe rail lane reaches Ireland — it stops on the continent.</li>
            <li>You price rail&rsquo;s 18–22 days without adding the ferry/feeder crossing and handling.</li>
            <li>You need a single-mode door-to-door move with no sea leg at all.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. Ports: China origin ports, Dublin, Cork and the short-sea question</h2>
    <p>
      The origin side is the familiar China port hierarchy: Shanghai and Ningbo-Zhoushan anchor the
      East China ocean services, while Shenzhen and Guangzhou cover South China and Qingdao, Tianjin
      and Xiamen provide northern/southeast alternatives. The destination side is Ireland&rsquo;s
      three-port geometry: <strong>Dublin (IEDUB)</strong> is the volume gate, <strong>Cork (IEORK)</strong>
      is the deep-water alternative on the south coast, and <strong>Rosslare</strong> serves the southeast.
      None of them is the whole story — the routing question is really which continental hub you
      transship at, because the final short-sea leg is what the ocean quote omits.
    </p>

