  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost composition and the hidden charges competitors miss</h2>
      <p>
        Ireland&rsquo;s statutory stack is only the visible top layer: <strong>EU common duty on CIF</strong>
        (typically 0–12% by HS code), then <strong>23% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</strong>. Below
        that line sit the charges unique to Ireland — the <strong>short-sea feeder or landbridge
        crossing</strong> and, if you still route via the UK, a <strong>second customs regime</strong> —
        plus the operational charges the verified snapshot does not quantify: THC, documentation,
        inspection, port storage, demurrage and detention. This is where a cheap freight quote becomes an
        expensive clearance.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Baseline statutory landed cost = (CIF + duty) × 1.23.</strong><br />
        Duty varies by HS code, so there is no single multiplier like the Gulf&rsquo;s 5%. For an
        illustrative USD 10,000 CIF shipment at an assumed 5% duty: USD 500 duty gives a USD 10,500
        duty-inclusive base, then 23% VAT of USD 2,415, giving <strong>USD 12,915</strong> before the
        short-sea leg and destination fees. The compound is <strong>1.05 × 1.23 = ×1.2915</strong>, not
        the 28% you would get by simply adding the two headline rates — a $115 difference on this base
        that most guides never show. Rates and duty lines are subject to change — verify the current duty
        line and VAT treatment with Irish Revenue before relying on this example.
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Full cost stack</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="The Rotterdam northern-range reference, EU duty and 23% VAT are MEDIUM/HIGH confidence from route/customs sources. Unquantified Irish and port fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish amounts."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — landed cost & customs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">What most guides skip</h3>
      <p>
        Five lines change the Irish landed cost and rarely appear on a standard ocean quote:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The short-sea feeder is not in the northern-range quote.</strong> A Rotterdam or
          Antwerp freight figure ends at the continental quay; the ~1–3 day feeder west to Dublin or Cork
          is a separate charge that must be added before the quote is usable.</li>
        <li><strong>The UK landbridge now costs a second customs regime.</strong> Pre-Brexit, transiting
          Britain was one move; post-Brexit it is two clearances (EU plus UK), which is why direct
          continental lo-lo services to Irish ports have grown since 2021.</li>
        <li><strong>23% VAT is four points above Germany&rsquo;s 19%.</strong> On the duty-inclusive base
          that is $420 more on a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty — and $315 more than the UK&rsquo;s 20% — before
          the short-sea leg is considered.</li>
        <li><strong>Dublin&rsquo;s tidal draft forces transshipment.</strong> The volume gate cannot take
          the largest mainline vessels, so the &ldquo;direct call&rdquo; many importers assume is often a
          northern-range transshipment in disguise — price the feeder explicitly.</li>
        <li><strong>VAT deferral is available.</strong> EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) defers
          import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — worth more cash at 23% than in a 19%
          or 20% market, and an underused working-capital lever for Irish importers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage vs detention: two clocks, two payees</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the charge the <strong>terminal</strong> applies when import cargo
        stays in the port beyond the allowed free time after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is the
        charge the <strong>ocean or feeder carrier</strong> applies when the importer keeps the container
        beyond the equipment free time after collection. They sound similar, but they are separate
        contracts, separate free-time allowances and separate per-day rates — and on a transshipment-heavy
        route like Ireland the detention clock can run against the feeder carrier as well as the mainline
        line, so a single move can carry two demurrage exposures and two detention exposures.
      </p>
      <p>
        Irish and Dublin/Cork free-time periods and per-day amounts are not published in the verified
        snapshot, so do not assume a Rotterdam or Antwerp schedule applies. Before booking, ask the
        carrier and the forwarder to put the Dublin/Cork free time, the feeder free time, demurrage rate
        and detention rate in writing. The most reliable avoidance tactic is documentary readiness:
        register the EORI early, lock the TARIC code, prepare the invoice/packing list/B-L and file the
        Irish declaration promptly so release happens before either free-time clock runs out.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Hidden charges to ask for on the quote</h3>
      <p>
        Request an itemised schedule that separates each destination line. A quote that only says &ldquo;freight
        + customs&rdquo; can hide <strong>THC</strong> at Dublin/Cork, the <strong>short-sea feeder</strong>,
        a <strong>documentation fee</strong>, a possible <strong>customs inspection fee</strong>, <strong>port
        storage</strong>, and the <strong>demurrage/detention</strong> exposure if release is delayed. Without
        those lines, a dollar-level freight quote cannot be compared or used for landed-cost budgeting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: Ireland duty, 23% VAT, EORI &amp; conformity</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      Ireland applies the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>, so duty is identical across member
      states and depends on the HS code — typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong>, with no
      China–EU free-trade agreement in force. Import <strong>23% VAT</strong> (13.5% and 9% reduced on
      some goods) is then charged on the <strong>duty-inclusive value</strong>, so the baseline statutory
      calculation is <code>(CIF + duty) × 1.23</code>. Specific goods can still carry different duty lines
      or VAT treatment, so the HS code remains the first compliance decision.
    </p>

    <h3>EORI — register before arrival</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong>EORI number</strong> is mandatory for any import into the EU and must be registered
      <em>before</em> the goods arrive. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for
      China-to-Ireland cargo, so start the registration before the vessel departs, not while it is
      discharging at Dublin or Cork.
    </p>

    <h3>TARIC classification and documents</h3>
    <p>
      Ireland classifies goods under the <strong>10-digit TARIC code</strong>, which drives the duty
      line, VAT treatment, permits and restricted screening. The standard document set is a commercial
      invoice, bill of lading (or CMR consignment note for road legs), packing list and the correct
      TARIC classification, backed by your EORI number. Confirm the current declaration workflow with a
      licensed Irish broker.
    </p>

    <h3>CE marking and REACH</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>CE marking</strong> is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated
      products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others. <strong>REACH</strong> is
      the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Ireland
      exactly as in every member state, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file
      before shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>VAT deferral via EU customs warehousing</h3>
    <p>
      Ireland, like other EU states, allows <strong>EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63)</strong> to
      defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for
      importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU. Given Ireland&rsquo;s 23% VAT tier, the
      deferral is worth more cash than in a 19% or 20% market, so ask your broker about the procedure
      before assuming VAT must be paid at the quay.
    </p>

    <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
    <p>
      SABER and SASO are <strong>Saudi-only</strong> conformity systems and do not apply to Ireland or the
      EU. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into an Irish quote. Ireland uses CE marking and REACH
      instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with Irish Revenue or your broker.
    </p>

    <h3>De-minimis and IOSS</h3>
    <p>
      The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro
      on most consignments. The <strong>€150</strong> figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the
      <strong>IOSS</strong> scheme simplifies VAT on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the
      current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with Irish Revenue before relying on
      these figures.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Ireland customs, duty, VAT & conformity" />
  </section>

