  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>At a 5% duty assumption, a $10,000 CIF shipment clears at $12,915: $500 duty (5% × 10,000), $2,415 VAT on the $10,500 duty-inclusive base (23% × 10,500), a 29.15% uplift (1.05 × 1.23 = 1.2915) — $420 above Germany's 19% VAT on the same base.</li>
      <li>Ireland's 23% VAT on CIF + duty equals 24.15% of CIF (23% × 1.05), so it adds $115 more than flat 23% on $10,000 CIF (2,415 − 2,300) — and every China→Ireland shipment then pays a short-sea crossing (Irish/Celtic Sea) that continental markets never do.</li>
      <li>There is no rail lane to arbitrage: the New Silk Road stops on the continent, leaving Ireland a binary sea-versus-air market — the 32–43-day sea window is the whole game, so a stockout risk can only be answered by air.</li>
      <li>The 15 CBM heuristic is fragile: at LCL $60–160/CBM, 15 CBM costs $900–2,400 (15 × 60 = 900; 15 × 160 = 2,400), already $1,200 above the $1,200 20ft FCL floor at the top of the LCL range — the true crossover sits at 7.5–20 CBM (1,200 ÷ 160 = 7.5; 1,200 ÷ 60 = 20).</li>
      <li>The short-sea feeder leg compounds LCL's per-CBM handling faster than FCL's per-container fees, so the 15 CBM crossover that holds on the continent is even lower in practice for Ireland — budget the crossing as a separate line, not a rounding error.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

